{"id": "q057", "question": "What is a binary heap, and which abstract data type is it most commonly used to implement?", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "A binary heap is a binary-tree data structure that is the most common implementation of the priority queue ADT; it is not a search tree but a hybrid between a binary tree and an array list.", "gold_evidence": ["A binary heap is a type of binary tree data structure that is also the most common implementation for the priority queue abstract data type", "A binary heap is not a search tree, but rather a hybrid data structure between a binary tree and an array list"], "source_file": "openstax_intro_cs_ch3_data_structures_algorithms.pdf", "tags": ["lang-en", "definition", "multi-evidence", "en-same-lang", "claude-drafted"], "reviewed": true}
{"id": "q058", "question": "How does a greedy algorithm differ from brute force when solving combinatorial problems?", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "Brute force generates all potential solutions, while a greedy algorithm repeatedly applies a simple rule to pick the next element and generates just one solution.", "gold_evidence": ["Unlike brute-force algorithms that solve combinatorial problems by generating all potential solutions, greedy algorithms instead focus on generating just one solution"], "source_file": "openstax_intro_cs_ch3_data_structures_algorithms.pdf", "tags": ["lang-en", "compare", "en-same-lang", "claude-drafted"], "reviewed": true}
{"id": "q059", "question": "In earned value analysis, what does earned value (EV) mean?", "answerable": true, "gold_answer": "EV, also called budgeted cost of work performed (BCWP), is the total of the planned values (PVs) for the work completed so far.", "gold_evidence": ["Earned value (EV) or Budgeted cost of work performed (BCWP) – total of PVs for the work completed at this time"], "source_file": "slides_spm_ch9_monitoring_and_control.pptx", "tags": ["lang-en", "definition", "en-same-lang", "claude-drafted"], "reviewed": true}
